<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">September 11 attacks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as 9/11</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The September 11 attacks were a coordinated series of suicide attacks carried out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 2001. A total of 19 hijackers seized four commercial airliners: two were flown into the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, which both collapsed, a third into the Pentagon in Arlington County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, and the fourth, United Airlines Flight 93, crashed in a field near Shanksville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, during a passenger revolt. The attacks killed 2,977 people. The United States responded by invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and launching its Global War on Terror, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> invoked Article 5 of the North Atlantic Treaty for the only time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/september-11-attacks.jpg" alt="September 11 attacks" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr user Michael Foran · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WTC_smoking_on_9-11.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osama-bin-laden">Osama bin Laden</a>, leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>, issued his first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a>, which declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and demanded the expulsion of all American troops stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>; he interpreted Muhammad as banning non-Muslims from Arabia and regarded the troop presence as a provocation to all Muslims. Al-Qaeda's cited motivations included United States support of Israel, American military bases in Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, whom the 2004 9/11 Commission Report named the principal architect of the attacks, had helped organise and finance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>'s 1993 bombing of the World Trade Center, and planned the Bojinka plot of January 1995 with his nephew Ramzi Yousef.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 11 September 2001 nineteen hijackers took control of four airliners, two Boeing 757s and two Boeing 767s, chosen because large planes on long flights carried more fuel. American Airlines Flight 11, flown by Mohamed Atta, struck the North Tower of the World Trade Center at 8:46 a.m., and United Airlines Flight 175, flown by Marwan al-Shehhi, struck the South Tower at 9:03 a.m. Both towers collapsed within an hour and forty-two minutes, destroying the other five structures of the complex. Hani Hanjour flew American Airlines Flight 77 into the Pentagon at 9:37 a.m.</p><p>United Airlines Flight 93, hijacked by Ziad Jarrah, was believed to be heading for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol">United States Capitol</a> or the White House. Alerted to the earlier attacks, the passengers revolted, and the hijackers crashed the aircraft into a field near Shanksville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, at 10:03 a.m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Counting the hijackers, the four crashes killed 2,996 people: 265 on the planes, 2,606 at the World Trade Center and in the surrounding area, and 125 at the Pentagon. They included 343 firefighters, 72 law enforcement officers and 55 military personnel, and more than 90 countries lost citizens. That evening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">Central Intelligence Agency</a> told President George W. Bush that the attacks were the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>. Bin Laden denied involvement until 2004, when excerpts of a taped statement accepting responsibility were released; he was killed in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> raid at Abbottabad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, on 2 May 2011.</p><p>The Federal Bureau of Investigation began PENTTBOM, the largest criminal inquiry in its history, and the 9/11 Commission, chaired by Thomas Kean, issued its 585-page report in July 2004. Many countries passed new anti-terrorism laws. The World Trade Center site was cleared by the end of May 2002, the Pentagon was rebuilt within a year, and One World Trade Center opened on 3 November 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “September 11 attacks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Qaeda formally declared the “far enemy” under attack in 1996, and Osama bin Laden’s terror attacks climaxed in the <b>September 11 attacks</b> of 2001.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Its popularity grew in the years after the <b>September 11 attacks</b>, when it was sung at sporting events beside the national anthem, and it has opened many Super Bowls and editions of WrestleMania.</blockquote><a href="/songs/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>September 11 attacks</b> Clear Channel Communications put it on a list of songs not to play.</blockquote><a href="/songs/imagine">Imagine</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2001, after the <b>September 11 attacks</b>, Leonard Slatkin replaced the usual pieces with a more serious programme, but they returned the following year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/land-of-hope-and-glory">Land of Hope and Glory</a></li><li><blockquote>It was little used during the Vietnam War but came back into use after the <b>September 11 attacks</b> and through the war on terror.</blockquote><a href="/songs/over-there">Over There</a></li></ul></section>
